            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Mix the soy sauce, mirin, sake, and sugar together in a small bowl. Stir until the sugar dissolves. Set aside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the oil in a large frying pan over medium-high heat. Place the chicken thighs skin-side down. Press them flat with a spatula."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Cook for 7 to 8 minutes without moving them. The skin should be deep golden and crisp. Turn the thighs over."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Cook on the flesh side for 5 minutes until cooked through. The juices should run clear when pierced with a skewer."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pour away any excess fat from the pan. Reduce the heat to medium-low."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pour the sauce into the pan. It will bubble vigorously. Spoon it over the chicken as it reduces. Cook for 3 to 4 minutes until the sauce is thick and coats the chicken in a shiny glaze."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Slice each thigh and arrange over steamed rice. Spoon the remaining glaze over the top. Scatter with sesame seeds and spring onions."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "The sauce burns before it thickens",
              "cause": "The heat was too high or the pan was too dry",
              "fix": "Keep the heat at medium-low once the sauce goes in. If the pan looks dry, add a splash of water and continue reducing."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "The skin is not crisp",
              "cause": "The pan was not hot enough or the skin was not pressed flat",
              "fix": "Make sure the pan is properly hot before the chicken goes in. Press the skin down firmly for the first minute of cooking."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "The chicken is cooked outside but raw inside",
              "cause": "The thighs were too thick or the heat was too high",
              "fix": "After turning, cover the pan with a lid for 3 minutes. This traps steam and cooks the thicker parts without burning the skin."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Teriyaki is found across Japan but it is especially popular as an everyday home-cooked dinner. The word combines teri, meaning shine or gloss, and yaki, meaning grilled or fried. Chicken teriyaki appears on school lunch menus, in bento boxes, and at family tables throughout the country. Outside Japan, it became one of the first Japanese dishes to gain widespread popularity in Britain and the United States."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}